# Backend API origin. Only VITE_-prefixed variables reach browser code — never put a secret
# here, since anything in this file ends up readable in the shipped JS bundle.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PRODUCTION (Vercel) — set in the Vercel dashboard, NOT here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=
#   Set it to the EMPTY STRING — present but with no value. Do NOT simply leave it unset.
#
#   With the Vercel Services setup in /vercel.json the frontend and backend share one domain, so
#   the correct production value is a relative base: an empty string makes api/client.js build
#   `/api/...`, which resolves same-origin and is routed to the backend service by the
#   `/api/(.*)` rewrite.
#
#   The distinction between "empty" and "unset" is real and was verified against a production
#   build of this app, not assumed. api/client.js reads
#       const BASE_URL = import.meta.env.VITE_API_BASE_URL;
#   with NO `?? ''` fallback, and Vite substitutes that expression at BUILD time:
#     - unset  -> compiles to `void 0` (undefined), so every call fetches the literal string
#                 "undefined/api/auth/login" — a relative path that 404s against the deployment.
#                 Every single API call breaks, with no build error and no type warning.
#     - empty  -> compiles to "", so every call fetches "/api/auth/login". Correct.
#
#   If a same-origin default is ever wanted WITHOUT depending on an env var being set correctly,
#   that needs a one-line code change in api/client.js (`?? ''`) — deliberately not made as part
#   of the deployment-config work, since it changes runtime behaviour.
#
#   A split deployment (frontend and backend on separate domains) is the other valid option; then
#   this takes the backend's full origin, e.g. https://<backend>.vercel.app, with no trailing
#   slash — client.js concatenates it directly onto paths that already start with "/".
